{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% import "_macros.html.j2" as m with context %}
{% block content %}
<article class="container ref-article ref-article--wide">
  {{ m.breadcrumb([('Home', '/'), ('Yeast', None)]) }}
  <header class="ref-head">
    <p class="ref-eyebrow">Reference · {{ yeast | length }} strains</p>
    <h1 class="ref-title">Yeast strains rated for thiol release (β-lyase activity)</h1>
    <p class="ref-lede">
      The same hop bill fermented with two different strains can smell like two different beers, because the enzyme that frees bound hop thiols, β-lyase, varies enormously between strains. This table rates every strain in Hoparoma's library on a three-step scale and links to the evidence.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="ref-section" aria-labelledby="scale-h">
    <h2 id="scale-h">The three levels</h2>
    <ul class="ref-legend">
      <li>{{ m.tier_badge('high') }} Thiol-forward strains: engineered or bred for β-lyase activity, or conventional strains that screened high. Expect free thiols above threshold when precursor-rich hops are used.</li>
      <li>{{ m.tier_badge('medium') }} London III and Conan lineage hazy strains and hybrid IPA strains. Moderate release; the classic NEIPA workhorses.</li>
      <li>{{ m.tier_badge('low') }} Chico-type clean strains, cool-fermented lager strains, kveik fermented hot, saison and Kölsch strains. Most bound precursors stay bound.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="ref-note">
      Screening studies find native β-lyase activity low in most brewing strains and clearly higher in a few English ale lineages; the engineered and bred thiol strains sit above all of them {{ m.cite('michel-2019') }} {{ m.cite('svedlund-2022') }} {{ m.cite('lallemand-biotransformation') }}. Lager yeast can release thiols too, from malt as well as hop precursors {{ m.cite('chenot-2022') }}. Levels are the labels Hoparoma shows in its yeast picker; the underlying coefficients are not published.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="ref-section" aria-labelledby="table-h">
    <h2 id="table-h">All strains</h2>
    <div class="ref-table-wrap">
      <table class="ref-table ref-table--index">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Strain</th>
            <th scope="col">Lab</th>
            <th scope="col">Type</th>
            <th scope="col">Temp °C (min · opt · max)</th>
            <th scope="col">Attenuation</th>
            <th scope="col">β-lyase level</th>
            <th scope="col">Availability</th>
            <th scope="col">Character tags</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {%- for y in yeast %}
          <tr id="{{ y.slug }}">
            <th scope="row">{% if y.has_page %}<a href="/yeast/{{ y.slug }}/">{{ y.display_name }}</a>{% else %}{{ y.display_name }}{% endif %}</th>
            <td>{{ y.lab }}</td>
            <td>{{ y.fermentation_type }}</td>
            <td>{{ y.temp_c.min | n }} · {{ y.temp_c.opt | n }} · {{ y.temp_c.max | n }}</td>
            <td>{{ (y.attenuation * 100) | n }} %</td>
            <td>{{ m.tier_badge(y.biotransformation_level) }}{% if y.biotransformation_level == 'high' and not y.has_page %} <span class="ref-conf ref-conf--estimate" title="Rated from supplier characterisation; literature note not yet on file">provisional</span>{% endif %}</td>
            <td>{{ m.availability_badge(y.availability) }}</td>
            <td class="ref-table__aroma">{{ y.style_tags | join(', ') }}</td>
          </tr>
          {%- endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="ref-note">
      Strains with a link have an evidence page. A level marked provisional comes from supplier characterisation that has not yet been written up here; treat it as a working rating.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="ref-section" aria-labelledby="pair-h">
    <h2 id="pair-h">Pairing with hops</h2>
    <p>
      High-level strains pay off with hops rated high for bound thiols:
      {% for h in thiol_hops %}<a href="{{ '/hops/' ~ h.slug ~ '/' if h.individual_page else '/hops/#' ~ h.slug }}">{{ h.name }}</a>{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.
      With low-thiol hops the strain choice matters less for thiols and more for esters and oil handling.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="ref-section ref-cta" aria-labelledby="app-h">
    <h2 id="app-h">In Hoparoma</h2>
    <p>Hoparoma models the yeast's release step together with hop precursors, malt precursors, and Phantasm dose, and warns when a low-activity strain is paired with a thiol-dependent recipe.</p>
    <a class="cta-pill" href="{{ cfg.app_store_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Get Hoparoma on the App Store</a>
    <p class="ref-note">Machine-readable copy: <a href="/data/yeast.json">yeast.json</a>.{% if cfg.dataset_license %} Published under <a href="{{ cfg.dataset_license }}" rel="license noopener" target="_blank">{{ cfg.dataset_license_name }}</a>: reuse freely with credit to Hoparoma and a link to hoparoma.com.{% endif %}</p>
  </section>
</article>
{% endblock %}
